# /etc/udev/rules.d/80-uinput.rules — installed by chezmoi
# (system/linux/etc/**, .chezmoiscripts/30-linux/install-system-10-files).
#
# Ubuntu's ydotool package ships /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/80-uinput.rules,
# which grants /dev/uinput access through a persistent GROUP/MODE
# assignment rather than the active-seat model this repository uses. udev
# reads rules.d directories in priority order (/etc highest, then /run,
# then /usr/lib lowest) and, when the SAME BASENAME exists in more than one
# directory, reads it ONLY from the highest-priority directory — it does
# not merge the two files. This file exists solely to occupy that basename
# with an inert override, so Ubuntu's package-owned rule never takes effect
# on a managed host.
#
# This file intentionally contains NO rule expression and NO
# GROUP/OWNER/MODE assignment — it grants no access at all. The
# repository's sole positive /dev/uinput grant is the active-seat
# TAG+="uaccess" rule in 70-uinput-solaar.rules, shared by Solaar and
# ydotoold. Fedora does not ship a package-owned 80-uinput.rules, so on
# Fedora this file is an inert defensive override with nothing to shadow.
#
# Do NOT add a rule expression, GROUP, OWNER, MODE, or persistent group
# membership here. If a future need arises for additional uinput behavior,
# add it to 70-uinput-solaar.rules (or a new file numbered below 73, per
# that file's ORDERING note) instead of resurrecting a grant in this
# basename.
